AB  - The objectives of this chapter are to evaluate recent evidence on the use of complementary or integrative medicine (CIM) in hospice and palliative care and summarize their potential benefits. From an earlier publication, a literature search was conducted in Embase, CINAHL, PsycINFO, and MEDLINE for studies evaluating CIM in the management of symptoms in the hospice and palliative settings from 1999 to 2016. A newer search using the same methods was conducted for the purposes of this chapter from 2016 to 2020. A total of 21 studies were evaluated. Seventeen studies were included from the earlier review while four newer studies were identified for inclusion. All studies evaluated a form of mind-body therapy. Massage therapy in combination with other CIM produced the most results. CIM may have modest, short-term benefits for improvement of symptoms experienced by patients in the palliative or hospice setting. More studies are needed to further clarify the role of CIM, using standardized methods and assessment tools. Patients and caregivers should discuss the risks and benefits of CIM with their health care providers. If patients decide to pursue CIM, the therapy should be conducted by a certified professional or a trained health care provider.
