AB  - In this chapter, I put together our recent data with published reports and present concepts and a rationale for our emerging hypothesis of a dysfunctional microbiome—vagus nerve—brain circuit in Alzheimer’s disease and associated diseases and medical conditions. Vagus nerve can be stimulated through different mechanisms. Tryptamine can affect vagus nerve via receptors including muscarinic, serotonin, and AhR receptors and via axons and synapses. Tryptamine is able to bind synaptic membranes and affect synaptic transmission presynaptically by influencing the transmitter release. Tryptamine can antagonize the glutamate-mediated activity of the vagal nerve. This chapter discusses the vagus nerve-related conditions including Arnold’s reflex, laryngopharyngeal reflux, gastroesophageal reflux, and related pulmonary diseases, and duodenogastric reflux. Production of biogenic amines including tryptamine fluctuates during fermentation started with microbe. The fluctuations in biological/biochemical processes depend on (1) nutrient fluctuations, (2) the fluctuations of the total acid, crude fat, amino acid nitrogen, total nitrogen, and ammonium salt, (3) the biofield effects, and (4) the position of the Earth with respect to other celestial bodies.
