KW  - Film appreciation
KW  - Emotional regulation
KW  - Non-clinical art healing
KW  - Interpretative phenomenological analysis
AB  - This qualitative study employed Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis (IPA) to explore the potential of film appreciation as a non-clinical, arts-based healing experience for supporting emotional regulation among undergraduate students. In this study, healing is understood not as clinical treatment or symptom reduction, but as a subjective, process-oriented experience involving emotional release, reflective meaning-making, and the partial restoration of psychological balance through engagement with film. Six participants aged 19–22 from diverse academic backgrounds were recruited through purposive sampling and took part in semi-structured, in-depth interviews. IPA revealed three interrelated experiential themes: (1) cinematic engagement as a catalyst for emotional release, (2) restored psychological balance following viewing experiences, and (3) perceived parallels between film-induced emotional shifts and the cathartic processes associated with expressive arts practices. Participants described how film narratives, imagery, and soundscapes enabled them not only to access and regulate difficult emotions, but also to reflect on personal concerns and reinterpret their experiences in ways that contributed to a renewed sense of well-being. This exploratory study contributes to the growing body of research on non-clinical arts-based engagement by offering phenomenological insights into how film appreciation may support emotional regulation, reflective coping, and meaning-making in everyday contexts. Practically, the findings highlight the potential of integrating film appreciation and related arts-based activities into higher education curricula as accessible and low-stigma approaches to supporting students’ mental well-being.
ER  - 

